t up with slurs and  insults directed at him in both his  housing block and at work in the LAPD  Fraser wants to free her people and  tells Kay that our lives mean nothing  next to the storm that's coming dying  for the right cause is the most human  thing we can do and although Fraser told  Kay the only way to protect the miracle  child was to kill Deckard he chooses not  to do that in fact just as Baz he saves  Deckard in the 1982 film kay does the  same in this one asserting his  independence and humanity wants more  just as he did when he lied to that  tenant Joshi about the fate of the  replicant baby still Fraser is  determined to reveal Rachel's baby to  the world when the time is right and  says the child will lead their army a  thread which could be continued in a  future movie although to be honest I'd  be happy if the story ended here rather  than trying to tie everything up too  neatly in future films earlier I  mentioned joy Kay's holographic  companion and she's a very interesting  part of the story even if what her  character says can often be a bit of a  red herring in terms of the final  resolution of the film joy is as the  adverts for her point out everything you  want to see and everything you want to  hear so when we first meet her she  switches through a number of different  outfits and looks until Casey is one he  likes and when she tells Kay that he's  special and important she's probably  just telling him  he wants to hear although I suspect that  Joey was set up as a very intelligent AI  who can figure out what our owner wants  and fulfill those needs there were some  hints in the movie that she might be  able to develop her own feelings and  possibly identity there are some clear  parallels between joy and Scarlett  Johansson's AI character Samantha from  Spike Jones's movie her for instance of  sex scenes in both movies although the  extent of Joy's real autonomy is left  more ambiguous in Blade Runner 2049 for  example when joy said I love you to Kay  just before love crushed and effectively  killed her was that real genuine emotion  or just what she was programmed to do in  other words she said what Kay wanted to  hear at that moment either way I thought  the performance of actress Anna the  Animus was cleverly ambiguous so that  were left wondering whether joy managed  to become truly sentient before she was  destroyed Neander Wallace who has  something of a god complex and calls his  replicant creations angels is also on  the hunt for the missing replicant child  he wants to create millions of new  replicants as he believes they are the  key to rebuilding the Earth's  environment and eventually colonizing  the stars  Wallace's view of history is that  civilizations only advanced when they  were willing to harness the power of a  slave population and he sees replicant  reproduction as a solution to creating  new replicants on mass Wallace has  always been willing to push the limits  of technology in pursuit of his own  agenda for instance he was the one who  got replicant prohibition repealed when  he developed a new model which he  demonstrated would always obey and never  rebel it's curious that Wallace has a  chip or socket in his neck and little  camera drones that float around in his  vicinity which presumably are there to  help him see or sense things his visual  impairments could be a metaphor for his  world view as although he understands  the power of replicants  he's blinkered and only wants to use  them for one purpose as slaves but even  his most trusted replicants like love  appear to have possible design defects  for example love seems to respond  emotionally in particularly stressful  situations such as crying just before  wallace slices open the belly of his  newborn female replicant or when love  realizes she has to kill Joshy  unlike love though Wallace clearly  appears to have survived the movie so  has the potential to reappear in a  future story or sequel by the way  Rachael'